{
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "A11Y-1",
      "severity": "medium",
      "category": "2.4.7 Focus Visible",
      "description": "public/styles.css:4-6 sets `*:focus { outline: none; }`, and no :focus or :focus-visible rule puts any indicator back (.star has border:none and background:none, lines 118-128). This change now moves focus on purpose: app.js:218-224 puts it back on the star after each redraw, and app.js:163/223 move it to the My Schedule button when the talk leaves the view. A sighted keyboard user can't see where focus landed, so they can't tell whether it is on the star they pressed or has jumped to the nav button. The rule existed before this change, and the change leans on it, so this is medium under the rubric. Smallest fix: delete the rule, or replace it with `:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #5b3fd6; outline-offset: 2px; }`. That colour gives 6.3:1 on white; any colour of at least 3:1 against #fff and #f6f5fb is fine."
    },
    {
      "id": "A11Y-2",
      "severity": "medium",
      "category": "1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum)",
      "description": "The clash warning comes straight after `.talk-when` (app.js:192), which holds the start and end times the clash is about. Its colour #b3b0c0 on the white card is 2.12:1 (styles.css:187-191, 12px), well under 4.5:1. Low-vision users can read the 'Overlaps with' warning (#92400e on white is 7.09:1, a pass) but not the times needed to settle the clash. This contrast failure existed before the change. Smallest fix: use #6b6880 for .talk-when (5.36:1 on white), the colour .talk-meta already uses. The same problem exists elsewhere on the page: .slot-time #c9c6d3 on #f6f5fb is 1.55:1, and .footer #b3b0c0 on #f6f5fb is 1.96:1."
    },
    {
      "id": "A11Y-3",
      "severity": "medium",
      "category": "1.4.11 Non-text Contrast",
      "description": "The star toggle is the control this change gives new focus handling and announcements to. Its glyph is #c9c6d3 on white when off (1.68:1) and #ffb020 on white when on (1.83:1) (styles.css:126, 133). Both are under 3:1, so low-vision users can't make out the control or its on/off state. This existed before the change. Smallest fix: use about #6b6880 for the off star and a darker amber such as #b45309 for the on star (5.02:1 on white)."
    },
    {
      "id": "A11Y-4",
      "severity": "medium",
      "category": "2.1.1 Keyboard",
      "description": "Clicking a talk card shows its abstract (app.js:208-212), but the card is a plain div: it has no tabindex, no role and no key handler, and nothing tells assistive technology whether the abstract is expanded. Keyboard and switch users can't open the abstract; screen-reader users only reach it because hidden text is skipped, so they don't get it either. This change adds clash styling and a warning to these cards but did not cause the problem. Smallest fix: put a real `<button aria-expanded>` for the disclosure inside the card, for example around the title, so it doesn't contain the star button."
    },
    {
      "id": "A11Y-5",
      "severity": "medium",
      "category": "2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum)",
      "description": "The star button is a 20px glyph with line-height 1, no border, no set size and only the browser's default padding (styles.css:118-128). That makes it about 22px tall, under 24px. The 'spacing' exception doesn't apply, because the star sits inside the clickable card (app.js:208), which is itself a pointer target. Tapping just outside the star opens the abstract instead of starring the talk, and users with tremors or motor impairments are the ones who miss. This existed before the change. Smallest fix: `.star { min-width: 24px; min-height: 24px; }`, or better 32px with padding."
    },
    {
      "id": "A11Y-6",
      "severity": "low",
      "category": "2.4.3 Focus Order",
      "description": "In My Schedule, un-starring a talk removes its card, and app.js:223 (and 163 when the list empties) sends focus back up to the My Schedule button in the nav. Focus isn't lost, but a keyboard user removing several talks has to Tab back down through the nav every time. Smallest fix: before redrawing, note the next star (or the previous one if there is none) and focus that; fall back to the My Schedule button only when the list is empty."
    },
    {
      "id": "A11Y-7",
      "severity": "low",
      "category": "1.3.1 Info and Relationships",
      "description": "The per-card warning (app.js:184-193) is a plain div that isn't linked to the star. A screen-reader user who moves through the page by Tab hears 'Add to My Schedule: <title>, toggle button' or 'Remove from…, pressed', with no hint that the talk clashes. The polite announcement only covers the moment of starring. Smallest fix: give the warning an id and set aria-describedby on the star to point at it."
    },
    {
      "id": "A11Y-8",
      "severity": "low",
      "category": "4.1.2 Name, Role, Value",
      "description": "The star's aria-label switches between 'Add to' and 'Remove from My Schedule' at the same time as its aria-pressed changes (app.js:199-203). Screen readers then read the state twice and contradict themselves: for example 'Remove from My Schedule: X, toggle button, pressed'. This change refocuses the star after every toggle, so users hear it every time. It existed before the change. Smallest fix: keep a fixed name such as 'Star: <title>' (or 'In My Schedule: <title>') and let aria-pressed alone carry the state."
    },
    {
      "id": "A11Y-9",
      "severity": "low",
      "category": "1.3.1 / 2.4.6 Headings and Landmarks",
      "description": "The new 'Schedule clashes' h2 (app.js:123) is the only heading on the page. The brand is a div rather than an h1, and the slot times are divs rather than headings. The clash region also sits outside <main> (index.html:20). Screen-reader users who navigate by heading find just this one heading, and nothing for the schedule's time slots. Smallest fix: make .brand an h1 and .slot-time an h2 (with the clashes heading as an h2 alongside them), and move #notices inside <main>."
    }
  ]
}
EOF
